  "title": "AtomWallet signature time-window (validUntil/validAfter) can be trivially bypassed by stripping the 12-byte suffix",
  "severity": "medium",
  "validity": "valid",
  "quality": "sufficient",
  "user_handle": "atilla055",
  "body": "## Vulnerability details\n\n##  Description\n\n  The _validateSignature function in AtomWallet implements an optional time-window mechanism via validUntil and validAfter fields appended as a 12-byte suffix to the ECDSA signature. The contract accepts two\n  signature formats:\n\n  - 65 bytes (r | s | v): interpreted as having no time restrictions (validUntil=0, validAfter=0)\n  - 77 bytes (r | s | v | validUntil | validAfter): parsed for time-window enforcement\n\n  The critical flaw is that the ECDSA signature covers only the userOpHash — the 12-byte time-window suffix is not included in the signed message. This means the suffix can be freely stripped or modified by any\n  third party without invalidating the cryptographic signature.\n\n  At line 297, the signed hash is derived exclusively from userOpHash:\n\n  bytes32 hash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", userOpHash));\n\n  The userOpHash is computed by the EntryPoint from the PackedUserOperation fields, but per ERC-4337 specification, the signature field itself is excluded from this hash. Therefore, the time-window bytes appended\n  inside the signature are never covered by any cryptographic commitment.\n\n  At lines 343-344, the extraction function treats a 65-byte signature as unrestricted:\n\n  if (signatureLength == 65) {\n      return (0, 0, signature); // validUntil=0 means no expiry, validAfter=0 means immediately valid\n  }\n\n  Any observer who sees a 77-byte time-restricted signature can truncate the last 12 bytes, producing a valid 65-byte signature that the contract accepts with zero time restrictions.\n\n## Impact\n\n  - Time-locked operations become permanently executable. An owner who intends a transaction to be valid only within a narrow window (e.g., 5 minutes) loses that guarantee entirely. The operation can be executed\n  at any arbitrary future time.\n  - Delayed execution attacks. A malicious bundler or MEV searcher can hold the stripped-signature UserOperation and execute it at the most disadvantageous moment — after a price movement, governance vote, access\n  revocation, or other state change the owner was trying to time-bound against.\n  - Breaks security model for all AtomWallets. Since AtomWallets are created per-atom and manage protocol assets across the Intuition ecosystem, the ability to bypass time restrictions impacts fund safety for\n  every atom.\n  - Not theoretical. ERC-4337 UserOperations are broadcast through an alt-mempool where bundlers and searchers routinely inspect, reorder, and modify operations before inclusion. The signature field is fully\n  visible and trivially modifiable.\n\n##   Attack Scenario\n\n  1. Wallet owner creates a time-restricted UserOperation (e.g., valid only between 14:00–14:05 UTC) and signs a 77-byte signature: [r(32) | s(32) | v(1) | validUntil(6) | validAfter(6)]\n  2. The UserOperation enters the ERC-4337 alt-mempool awaiting bundler inclusion\n  3. A malicious bundler or MEV searcher extracts the 77-byte signature, truncates it to 65 bytes (removing the time-window suffix), and replaces userOp.signature with the shortened version\n  4. The contract's _extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature sees a 65-byte signature and returns (validUntil=0, validAfter=0) — no time restriction\n  5. _validateSignature recovers the same signer (since the ECDSA bytes are identical) and returns _packValidationData(false, 0, 0)\n  6. The EntryPoint accepts the operation at any time, completely bypassing the owner's intended time window",
  "proof_of_concept": "##  Proof of Concept\n\n  // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n  pragma solidity 0.8.29;\n\n  import \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\n  contract TimeWindowBypassPoC is Test {\n\n      /// @dev Mirrors AtomWallet._extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature exactly\n      function _extract(bytes memory signature)\n          internal\n          pure\n          returns (uint48 validUntil, uint48 validAfter, bytes memory rawSignature)\n      {\n          uint256 signatureLength = signature.length;\n          if (signatureLength == 65) {\n              return (0, 0, signature);\n          }\n          require(signatureLength == 77, \"Invalid sig length\");\n\n          uint256 metaOffset = signatureLength - 12;\n          rawSignature = new bytes(65);\n          for (uint256 i = 0; i < 65; i++) {\n              rawSignature[i] = signature[i];\n          }\n\n          bytes memory meta = new bytes(12);\n          for (uint256 i = 0; i < 12; i++) {\n              meta[i] = signature[metaOffset + i];\n          }\n\n          uint256 word;\n          assembly {\n              word := mload(add(meta, 32))\n          }\n          uint96 packed = uint96(word >> 160);\n          validUntil = uint48(packed >> 48);\n          validAfter = uint48(packed);\n      }\n\n      function test_timeWindowBypass() public pure {\n          // Step 1: Create a 65-byte ECDSA signature (r, s, v)\n          bytes memory rawSig = new bytes(65);\n          for (uint256 i = 0; i < 65; i++) {\n              rawSig[i] = bytes1(uint8(i + 1));\n          }\n\n          // Step 2: Owner appends time-window → 77-byte signature\n          uint48 intendedValidUntil = 1700000100;\n          uint48 intendedValidAfter = 1700000000;\n          bytes memory fullSig = abi.encodePacked(rawSig, intendedValidUntil, intendedValidAfter);\n          assertEq(fullSig.length, 77, \"Full signature must be 77 bytes\");\n\n          // Step 3: Verify 77-byte signature parses time restrictions correctly\n          (uint48 vu1, uint48 va1, bytes memory ecdsa1) = _extract(fullSig);\n          assertEq(vu1, intendedValidUntil, \"validUntil must match\");\n          assertEq(va1, intendedValidAfter, \"validAfter must match\");\n          assertEq(ecdsa1.length, 65, \"Extracted ECDSA must be 65 bytes\");\n\n          // Step 4: ATTACK — strip the 12-byte suffix\n          bytes memory strippedSig = new bytes(65);\n          for (uint256 i = 0; i < 65; i++) {\n              strippedSig[i] = fullSig[i];\n          }\n\n          // Step 5: Verify stripped signature has NO time restrictions\n          (uint48 vu2, uint48 va2, bytes memory ecdsa2) = _extract(strippedSig);\n          assertEq(vu2, 0, \"validUntil bypassed to 0 (no expiry)\");\n          assertEq(va2, 0, \"validAfter bypassed to 0 (immediately valid)\");\n          assertEq(ecdsa2.length, 65, \"Extracted ECDSA must be 65 bytes\");\n\n          // Step 6: Confirm the ECDSA bytes are IDENTICAL — same signer recovers\n          assertEq(keccak256(ecdsa1), keccak256(ecdsa2), \"ECDSA bytes must be identical\");\n      }\n  }\n\n  Run: forge test --match-test test_timeWindowBypass -vv\n\n  The PoC proves:\n  1. A 77-byte signature with validUntil=1700000100, validAfter=1700000000 correctly parses time restrictions\n  2. Truncating to 65 bytes produces validUntil=0, validAfter=0 — zero restrictions\n  3. The extracted ECDSA signature is byte-identical in both cases, so ECDSA.tryRecover returns the same signer\n\n## Recommended Mitigation\n\n  Include the time-window parameters in the signed hash so they become cryptographically bound:\n\n  function _validateSignature(\n      PackedUserOperation calldata userOp,\n      bytes32 userOpHash\n  ) internal virtual override returns (uint256 validationData) {\n      (uint48 validUntil, uint48 validAfter, bytes memory signature) =\n          _extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature(userOp.signature);\n\n      // Bind time-window to the signed message\n      bytes32 hash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\n          \"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\",\n          keccak256(abi.encodePacked(userOpHash, validUntil, validAfter))\n      ));\n\n      (address recovered, ECDSA.RecoverError recoverError, bytes32 errorArg) =\n          ECDSA.tryRecover(hash, signature);\n      // ... remainder unchanged\n  }\n\n  This ensures that stripping or modifying the time-window bytes invalidates the ECDSA signature, making the two formats cryptographically distinct.\n\n ## Assessed type\n\n  Access Control",
